id: rule-array-read-bounds
title: With the bounds
topic: Arrays
example-of: ARRAY-READ
derived: true

task:
  Use the rule ARRAY-READ to decide whether this triple can be derived.

given:
  Assignment expression: $a[i]$

pre: a[i] > 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
post: x > 0

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  x := a[i]      // r
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The substitution
rule: ARRAY-READ
focus: r
text:
  As with the rule ASSIGN, we substitute the variable $x$ with the
  assignment expression $a[i]$ in $Q$.
calc:
  (x > 0)[x <- a[i]]     // Q is x > 0
  = a[i] > 0             // x replaced by a[i]
set: R1 = a[i] > 0

step: The read must be defined
rule: ARRAY-READ
focus: r
text:
  The expression reads the array at position $i$, so the rule adds
  $def(a[i])$: position $i$ must exist.
calc:
  def(a[i])
  = 0 <= i < sizeof(a)   // the positions of a are 0, ..., sizeof(a) - 1
set: R1 = a[i] > 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)

step: P implies R1
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R1
text:
  >>> «P» ==> «R1»
vc: a[i] > 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) ==> a[i] > 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
why:
  The two formulas are the same, so the rule derives the triple.
